      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we start by reducing the angle to an equivalent angle within the first circle (i.e., within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\)). This can be done by subtracting \\(2\\pi\\) (which is \\(\\frac{6\\pi}{3}\\)) repeatedly until the angle is within the desired range.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is equivalent to \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) within the first circle. Now, we need to find \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe angle \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{6\\pi}{3} - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nIn the fourth quadrant, the sine of an angle is negative. Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Thus:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
