      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we need to first simplify the angle to an equivalent angle within the standard range of \\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\). We do this by subtracting multiples of \\(2\\pi\\) (which is \\(6\\pi/3\\)) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is equivalent to \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) in the standard range. Next, we need to find \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant, where the sine function is negative. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{5\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe sine of \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) is the same as the sine of \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\), which is:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
